<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhulbahante</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dhulbahante are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clan of the Harti branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darod">Darod</a>, living mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sool">Sool</a> region and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaag">Sanaag</a> and Togdheer. Their territory lies within the borders of former British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, but many Dhulbahante have rejected the authority of Somaliland, and their lands have been contested between Somaliland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntland">Puntland</a> and, from 2023, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ssc-khatumo">SSC-Khatumo</a> administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhulbahante.jpg" alt="Dhulbahante" width="311" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D9%91%D8%AF_%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%91%D9%87_%D8%AD%D8%B3%D9%86.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>By tradition the clan descends from Said Saleh Abdi Mohamed Abdirahman bin Isma'il al-Jabarti, known as Dhulbahante, who is buried at Badweyn; his mother is said to have come from the Arap clan of the Isaaq, a link that has encouraged an affinity between the two clans. The clan's homeland straddles the Haud and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nugaal-valley">Nugaal Valley</a>, and those who settle on either plateau are called the Reer Hawd and the Reer Nugaaled. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> the Dhulbahante live almost entirely in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sool">Sool</a> region, and also in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erigavo">Erigavo</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaag">Sanaag</a> and Buuhoodle district of Togdheer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The clan is led by hereditary chiefs with the title of Garad, a title first used by the ancestor Garad Shirshore and most widespread among the Dhulbahante, who have 13 active Garads. Its two major sub-clans are the Mohamoud Garad and the Farah Garad, while the others form a loose confederation called the Baho Nugaaled. The anthropologist I.M. Lewis counted 50 groups that pay diyya, blood money for their members, gathered into four lineages of unequal size.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 19th century the explorer Cruttenden described the Dhulbahante as horsemen armed with two spears and a shield, whose horses were held to descend from the stud of Suleiman. Most of the fighters of the Dervish Movement came from the clan, whose members were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> herders and warriors and had signed no treaty of protection with the British; those of Buuhoodle were its first and most persistent supporters. Garad Ali Garad Mohamoud, who wished to stay autonomous under neither the British nor the Dervishes, quarrelled with Sayyid Mohamed Abdullah Hassan, who ordered his assassination.</p><p>In 1998 the Dhulbahante joined other Harti clans in founding the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntland">Puntland</a>, and voters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sool">Sool</a> were less supportive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>'s 2001 referendum on its constitution and independence. After Somaliland took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/las-anod">Las Anod</a> in 2007, the clan's chiefs met at Boocame in November 2007, rejected Somaliland's secession and demanded the withdrawal of its forces. In 2009 the SSC Movement, led by Saleban Essa Ahmed, was founded to remove Somaliland from Dhulbahante territories.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhulbahante” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Puntland, the autonomous region of Somalia founded in 1998, disputes the Sool and Sanaag regions, whose <b>Dhulbahante</b> and Warsangeli clans are divided in their loyalties, and Somaliland forces took the town of Las Anod in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Military</a></li></ul></section>
